        "title": "Philosophy of Religion",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "**Aristotle/Aquinas Version** (the \"five ways\"):\nAquinas's First Way: things are moved; nothing moves itself; there cannot be an\ninfinite regress of movers; therefore there is an unmoved mover. Similar for\ncausation (Second Way) and contingent existence (Third Way, the \"argument from\ncontingency\")."
    },
    {
      "id": "05-PHILOSOPHY-RELIGION.md#009",
      "path": [
        "05-PHILOSOPHY-RELIGION.md"
      ],
      "level": 0,
      "line": 1,
      "metadata": {
        "backsource_ids": "[mdloom-backfill:religious-studies:05-philosophy-religion, git-history:religious-studies:05-philosophy-religion]",
        "canonical_path": "religious-studies/05-PHILOSOPHY-RELIGION.md",
        "concepts": "[philosophy, religion]",
        "current_path": "religious-studies/05-PHILOSOPHY-RELIGION.md",
        "id": "maxim:religious-studies:philosophy-religion",
        "index_roles": "[guide, root-concept]",
        "kind": "guide",
        "maxim_schema": "maxim.frontmatter.v1",
        "module": "religious-studies",
        "remap_from": "[]",
        "remap_to": "[]",
        "root_concepts": "[philosophy, religion]",
        "section": "religious-studies",
        "source_custody": "partial",
        "status": "source-custody",
        "title": "Philosophy of Religion",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "The logical form:\n```\n  1. Everything that exists has a cause.\n  2. The universe exists.\n  3. Therefore the universe has a cause.\n  4. That cause is God.\n```"
    },
    {
      "id": "05-PHILOSOPHY-RELIGION.md#010",
      "path": [
        "05-PHILOSOPHY-RELIGION.md"
      ],
      "level": 0,
      "line": 1,
      "metadata": {
        "backsource_ids": "[mdloom-backfill:religious-studies:05-philosophy-religion, git-history:religious-studies:05-philosophy-religion]",
        "canonical_path": "religious-studies/05-PHILOSOPHY-RELIGION.md",
        "concepts": "[philosophy, religion]",
        "current_path": "religious-studies/05-PHILOSOPHY-RELIGION.md",
        "id": "maxim:religious-studies:philosophy-religion",
        "index_roles": "[guide, root-concept]",
        "kind": "guide",
        "maxim_schema": "maxim.frontmatter.v1",
        "module": "religious-studies",
        "remap_from": "[]",
        "remap_to": "[]",
        "root_concepts": "[philosophy, religion]",
        "section": "religious-studies",
        "source_custody": "partial",
        "status": "source-custody",
        "title": "Philosophy of Religion",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "Immediate problems: (1) is questioned (does everything need a cause, or just\ncontingent things?). The move from \"cause of the universe\" to \"God\" requires\nsubstantial additional argument. Russell's response (BBC debate with Copleston,\n1948): \"The universe is just there, and that's all.\" Why does the universe need\na cause if God doesn't?"
    },
    {
      "id": "05-PHILOSOPHY-RELIGION.md#011",
      "path": [
        "05-PHILOSOPHY-RELIGION.md"
      ],
      "level": 0,
